<h2 id="3-18-taf-terminal-aerodrome-forecast"><strong>3.18 TAF: Terminal Aerodrome Forecast</strong></h2>
<p>A TAF is a coded forecast for conditions expected in the vicinity of a terminal area during a specified valid period. It uses many of the same abbreviations as a METAR, but it includes forecast-change groups such as FM (from), TEMPO (temporary), and BECMG (becoming).</p>
<p>TAFs are useful for determining whether conditions are expected to improve or deteriorate during a mission window. As with all airport-based products, the remote PIC must consider whether local terrain and distance make the forecast representative of the actual UAS operating site.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/annotated-taf.jpg"><img src="/assets/part107/textbook/annotated-taf.jpg" alt="Annotated South Bend TAF training example; the report text and interpretation follow." loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>TAF example from the presentation, annotated to show issue time, validity, wind, visibility, ceilings, and change groups (source slide 38).</p><p><a href="/assets/part107/textbook/annotated-taf.jpg">Open original figure at full size</a></p>
<p><strong>Report text shown in the figure</strong></p><pre>TAF KSBN 171145Z 171212 VRB03KT 2 SM SCT030 BKN080 TEMPO 1517 SHRA
FM2200 13009KT 1SM OVC030 BECMG0210 NSW BKN030</pre>
<p><strong>Figure description:</strong> The supplied slide identifies South Bend, an issue time on the 17th at 1145 UTC, and a validity period from 1200 to 1200. It describes variable wind at 3 knots, 2 statute miles visibility, scattered clouds at 3,000 feet, and broken clouds at 8,000 feet, with temporary rain showers from 1500 to 1700. From 2200 it gives wind from 130 degrees at 9 knots, 1 statute mile visibility, and overcast at 3,000 feet. Its final explanation gives no significant weather and broken clouds at 3,000 feet between 0200 and 1000. This is the historical training example as supplied, not a current forecast.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="3-19-sigmets-convective-sigmets-and-graphical-aviation-weather"><strong>3.19 SIGMETs, Convective SIGMETs, and Graphical Aviation Weather</strong></h2>
<p>SIGMETs identify significant weather hazards that can affect aircraft. Convective SIGMETs are associated with hazardous convective weather such as severe thunderstorms, embedded thunderstorms, lines of thunderstorms, and other organized convection meeting issuance criteria. A small UAS should not be operated in an area affected by severe convective weather.</p>
<p>Current Aviation Weather Center products also include Graphical Forecasts for Aviation (GFA), G-AIRMETs, radar, satellite imagery, METAR/TAF displays, SIGMETs, icing, turbulence, wind, ceiling, visibility, and other layers. These graphical products make it easier to see how hazards relate spatially to a planned operating area.</p>
<p><strong>2026 UPDATE  </strong>The Aviation Weather Center discontinued CONUS text AIRMETs in January 2025; G-AIRMETs are now the primary CONUS AIRMET-format advisory product. Students may still encounter older references in legacy study material.</p>
<h2 id="3-20-weather-decision-making-for-a-remote-pic"><strong>3.20 Weather Decision Making for a Remote PIC</strong></h2>
<p>Weather information is useful only if it changes decisions. A remote PIC should establish personal and aircraft-specific limits before arriving at the field. Those limits may be more conservative than the regulatory minimums.</p>
<ul>
<li>Know the manufacturer&#x27;s wind and precipitation limitations.</li>
<li>Consider gusts, not just sustained wind.</li>
<li>Evaluate whether the aircraft can return upwind with adequate battery reserve.</li>
<li>Watch temperature-dew point spread for fog and low-cloud potential.</li>
<li>Avoid thunderstorms and rapidly developing convection.</li>
<li>Consider density altitude when operating hot, high, or heavily loaded.</li>
<li>Recheck conditions during the flight; weather can change during even a short mission.</li>
<li>Land early when conditions approach the limit instead of waiting until the operation becomes an emergency.</li>
</ul>
<p class="textbook-callout"><strong>SCENARIO  </strong>A mission begins with wind at 8 knots and gusts to 12. Twenty minutes later gusts reach 20 knots and the aircraft is returning slowly against the wind. The correct decision is not to &#x27;use the remaining battery.&#x27; Terminate the mission and recover while adequate energy and control margin remain.</p>
<h2 id="3-21-chapter-summary"><strong>3.21 Chapter Summary</strong></h2>
<ul>
<li>Unequal heating of Earth&#x27;s surface drives pressure differences, convection, and many weather processes.</li>
<li>Thermals form over stronger heat sources and can create vertical currents and turbulence.</li>
<li>Wind and wind shear can reduce UAS control margin and endurance; local terrain may make site conditions differ from airport reports.</li>
<li>Fronts separate different air masses. Frontal passage commonly brings temperature, pressure, and wind changes.</li>
<li>Unstable air favors turbulence, cumuliform clouds, and showery precipitation; stable air favors stratiform clouds, fog, haze, and smoother conditions.</li>
<li>Part 107 requires at least 3 statute miles of flight visibility, 500 feet below clouds, and 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds.</li>
<li>Thunderstorms require moisture, instability, and lift. The mature stage is usually the most intense.</li>
<li>Icing increases drag and weight while reducing lift and thrust. Frost should be removed before flight.</li>
<li>Fog commonly forms when temperature and dew point converge.</li>
<li>High density altitude means thin air and reduced aircraft performance.</li>
<li>METARs report observed weather; TAFs forecast terminal weather.</li>
<li>Use current FAA/Aviation Weather Center tools to evaluate hazards and trends before and during UAS operations.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="3-22-knowledge-check"><strong>3.22 Knowledge Check</strong></h2>
<ol>
<li>What fundamental process drives most weather on Earth?</li>
<li>What is a thermal?</li>
<li>Which generally produces stronger thermals: dry plowed ground or a moist marsh?</li>
<li>Define wind shear.</li>
<li>Name three common indications of frontal passage.</li>
<li>Which generally moves faster, a cold front or a warm front?</li>
<li>What type of clouds are commonly associated with unstable air?</li>
<li>What type of clouds are commonly associated with stable air?</li>
<li>What is a temperature inversion?</li>
<li>What is the Part 107 minimum flight visibility?</li>
<li>What are the Part 107 cloud-clearance requirements?</li>
<li>What three ingredients are required for thunderstorm development?</li>
<li>During which thunderstorm stage are updrafts and downdrafts both present?</li>
<li>What change signals that a growing thunderstorm cell is entering the mature stage?</li>
<li>What two basic conditions are necessary for structural icing?</li>
<li>What happens to lift and drag when ice accumulates on an aircraft?</li>
<li>What weather relationship indicates that fog may soon form?</li>
<li>What conditions favor radiation fog?</li>
<li>What is required for advection fog to form?</li>
<li>What conditions increase density altitude?</li>
<li>How does high density altitude affect aircraft performance?</li>
<li>Is a METAR an observation or a forecast?</li>
<li>What does 12008KT mean in a METAR?</li>
<li>What does BKN010 mean?</li>
<li>What does A3005 mean?</li>
<li>What is a TAF?</li>
<li>What does TEMPO indicate in a TAF?</li>
<li>What kind of weather is associated with a Convective SIGMET?</li>
<li>Why might the weather at an airport differ from the weather at a UAS operating site several miles away?</li>
<li>Why should a remote PIC establish weather limits more conservative than the legal minimums?</li>
</ol>
<h3 id="answer-key"><strong>Answer Key</strong></h3>
<ol>
<li>Unequal heating of Earth&#x27;s surface.</li>
<li>A rising column of warmer air produced by convective heating.</li>
<li>Dry plowed ground.</li>
<li>A rapid change in wind speed, direction, or both over a small distance.</li>
<li>Temperature change, pressure trend change, and a shift in wind direction and/or speed.</li>
<li>A cold front.</li>
<li>Cumuliform clouds.</li>
<li>Stratiform clouds and fog.</li>
<li>A layer in which temperature increases with altitude; it tends to suppress vertical mixing.</li>
<li>3 statute miles.</li>
<li>At least 500 feet below and 2,000 feet horizontally from clouds.</li>
<li>Moisture, instability, and a lifting mechanism.</li>
<li>The mature stage.</li>
<li>Precipitation beginning to fall from the cloud base, indicating downdraft development.</li>
<li>Visible liquid moisture and a surface temperature at or below freezing.</li>
<li>Lift decreases and drag increases; weight also increases and thrust/control margin may decrease.</li>
<li>Air temperature and dew point becoming equal or very close.</li>
<li>Clear sky, moist air, light/calm wind, and nighttime surface cooling.</li>
<li>Wind carrying warm moist air over a colder surface.</li>
<li>High elevation, low pressure, high temperature, and high humidity.</li>
<li>It reduces lift, thrust, climb performance, and overall performance margin.</li>
<li>An observation.</li>
<li>Wind from 120 degrees true at 8 knots.</li>
<li>Broken cloud layer at 1,000 feet AGL.</li>
<li>Altimeter setting 30.05 inches of mercury.</li>
<li>A Terminal Aerodrome Forecast for expected weather in the terminal area during a defined valid period.</li>
<li>Temporary conditions are forecast during the stated time period.</li>
<li>Significant convective weather such as severe, embedded, or organized thunderstorms.</li>
<li>Terrain, surface heating, buildings, vegetation, elevation, and distance can produce local weather differences.</li>
<li>Legal minimums do not account for the performance limits of every aircraft, mission, pilot, or operating site.</li>
</ol>
